@Numeric formats
Numeric formats are used in screens to display the contents of numeric
variables to the player during the game.

On this screen, select which numeric format that you want to edit by the
"Numeric format edit" field; this is a number from 0 to 15.

Next is the selection of the type of numeric format. After that are three
additional parameters for controlling the formatting; "Lead", "Mark", and
"Division"; their meaning depends on the type of format, which is explained
below. Division is the number to divide the value by before interpreting it
in this format; in most cases, division rounds down, although there are
some exceptions as noted below.

The following types of numeric formats are available. The ones with "ext."
are used for extended digit positions; 16 is added to the digit position
coded in the screen definition.

$Decimal
Display a number in decimal. Position 0 is the ones digit (on the right).
Lead denotes the leading characters to display; for example, "0" will cause
it to display leading zeros and a space will hide them. Note that you must
define one format to include a leading zero if you want that digit position
to always show a digit; if you do not, then no digits will be displayed if
the value is zero.

$Uppercase hex / Lowercase hex
Display a number in hexadecimal (base sixteen). Lead works as for decimal.

$Octal
Display a number in octal (base eight). Lead works as for decimal.

$Comma
Display commas for decimal numbers. Position is interpreted as for decimal;
Mark is displayed if there should be a digit there or Lead if not.

$Roman
Display roman numbers. Position 0 is the first letter (on the left). Mark
is the code for the letter "A"; you may use this to switch between uppercase
and lowercase. Lead is used to display excess space. Division is rounded to
a multiple of one half; if one half remains then "S" is displayed.

$œsd money
Display the old English money. Digit positions are right to left (same as
for Decimal, Octal, etc), and Lead is interpreted as for decimal numbers.
The value is interpreted as a number of pence, although farthings are also
possible if the division is 2 or 4. Position 0 is farthings; if division is
2 then Mark is used for a half penny, but if division is 4 then it is a
plain number of farthings. Positions 1 and 2 are pence. Positions 3 and
4 are shillings. Other positions are pounds. Note that this does not
automatically add any delimiters; you must add those by yourself.

$Meter (full blocks)
Show a meter. Division is rounding up for this interpretation. Mark is
shown if the position (after division) is less than the value, or Lead is
displayed otherwise. This can be used for meters in any direction, including
unusual directions such as spirals. Use "Meter ext." if sixteen positions is
not enough and you need a longer meter.

$Meter (half blocks)
Show a meter, like for full blocks, but each position can show two values.
The Mark is the character for a half-full block; the character for a full
block is always "Û".

$Binary
Position is the bit number (0 for the low bit); shows Mark if the bit is
set or Lead if the bit is clear.

$Character
Division is ignored; the value is the character code to display. If the
value is zero then the Lead is displayed. Position is how many bytes to
shift right to find the character code to be displayed.

$Nonzero
Show Mark if the value is nonzero or Lead if the value is zero. Division
can be set to the minimum value for which Mark is displayed. Position is
ignored for this format.

$Board name
Displays the board name whose number is the given value; ignores division.
Position 0 is the first character of the board name. If there are more
positions than the length of the board name, then Lead is displayed.

